REMOVED_CASE_ID PAYNTER MARTHA  P  49 F  REG REF REMOVED_DATE    REMOVED_CASE_ID  WIC FINN LEONARD M  M D                                                                                             Specimen  REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID REMOVED_CASE_ID SOUT Received  REMOVED_DATE  1324 Spec Type    Clinical diagnosis   history  5 mm  cluster of indeterminate calcifications  left posterior upper outer quadrant    fibrocystic changes vs ductal carcinoma in situ Edited by  REMOVED_DATE    1325 PAT MURJ Gross description  The specimen is received in formalin in one container  labeled  stereotactic loc reduced SVC  left breast calcifications  and consists of two cassettes  The first  which is purple in color and designated on the specimen requisition to be the upper outer quadrant with calcifications  consists of three firm  yellow  glistening needle biopsies measuring 2 cm  in length x REMOVED_DATE cm  in diameter to 3 cm  in length x REMOVED_DATE cm  in diameter  3X all  labeled 1  The second  which is yellow in color and designated on the specimen requisition to be the remainder of the tissue  consists of a REMOVED_DATE x REMOVED_DATE x REMOVED_DATE cm  aggregate of yellowish red  firm needle biopsies measuring REMOVED_DATE cm  in diameter each  2 agg  all  labeled REMOVED_DATE  Dictated by  LV Edited by  REMOVED_DATE    1338 PAT MURJ DIAGNOSIS  Left breast  posterior upper outer quadrant  needle core biopsies  Microcalcifications identified and associated with atypical ductal hyperplasia  columnar type  see note   Edited by  REMOVED_DATE    1025 PAT DORD REMOVED_DATE    1150 PAT MURJ DESCRIPTIVE COMMENT  NOTE  This case was sent to dr  Fritz Koerner at Massachusetts General Hospital  He concurs and adds  The tissue in the background of this core biopsy specimen shows stromal atrophy  You have marked a small duct and several terminal duct lobular units distended by a population of atypical ductal cells  They appear enlarged and they possess slightly enlarged  smoothly contoured  oval or round nuclei  homogeneous chromatin  inconspicuous nucleoli  and modest amounts of eosinophilic  apical cytoplasm  Most of the cells grow in a single  sometimes crowded or pseudostratified layer  however  those in a few foci seem to show genuine stratification and the formation of miniature micropapillary tufts  The presence of these cytological alterations identifies the cells as atypical  and the crowding and stratification of the cells imply a significant degree of proliferation of the atypical population  Because of these findings  I would not hesitate to make the diagnosis of atypical ductal hyperplasia of columnar cell type  I share your concerns about the frequent lack of significant findings in specimens excised because of a diagnosis of atypical ductal hyperplasia  Approximately sixty percent of our cases showing atypical columnar cell hyperplasia on a core biopsy specimen fall into this category  Perhaps we will learn criteria that will allow us to reduce this percentage  but I would not back away from the diagnosis of atypical ductal hyperplasia in the case at hand   Final Diagnosis by  Nicole A  Mihok  M D  Edited by  REMOVED_DATE    1157 PAT MURJ Notes  REMOVED_DATE  Received all 9 slides and report from Dr Winer via mail  NAM td REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID REMOVED_DATE  Sent all nine slides to Dr  Eric Winer   DFCI via courier at the req of Leanna NWH Breast Center  jam  REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID   617  REMOVED_DATE  Dr Winer  REMOVED_DATE  Rec d all nine slides from Dr  Koerner  dg CR REMOVED_DATE  Second report faxed from MGH  jam REMOVED_DATE  Rec d path report from Dr  Koerner via fax  dg REMOVED_DATE  Sent nine slides  1 1L 3L  REMOVED_DATE L 3L and REMOVED_DATE L 3L  to Dr  Frederick Koerner   MGH via courier for opinion NAM jam  CO   617  REMOVED_DATE Edited by  REMOVED_DATE    1156 PAT MURJ REMOVED_DATE    1125 PAT GILD REMOVED_DATE    1251 PAT MURJ REMOVED_DATE    1403 PAT GILD REMOVED_DATE    0731 PAT MURJ REMOVED_DATE    1339 PAT CORCCODES  